    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Frankford’s first gristmill, built in the 1660s, marked the neighborhood’s beginnings as a manufacturing hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankford%2C_Philadelphia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Historical Society of Frankford, founded in 1905, preserves the area’s history through lectures, collections, and community programs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        13,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thehistoricalsocietyoffrankford.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Frankford’s name traces back to a 1682 Quaker settlement that replaced earlier Dutch, Swedish, Finnish, and Native American communities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://philadelphiabeautiful.com/frankford/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Grand Army of the Republic Museum and Library, alongside the Historical Society, highlights Frankford’s rich cultural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://philadelphiabeautiful.com/frankford/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A 1773 grist mill on the creek, funded by Quaker Nicholas Waln, was operated by Mary Peters, a pioneering female trader.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankford%2C_Philadelphia"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Frankford’s historic division split the neighborhood into Frankford proper and East Frankford, the latter lying east of Frankford Avenue.",
